# project-config.md
Last-Updated: 2025-06-07

## Project Goal
This project is an event-driven, multi-channel recruitment communication middleware. It ingests candidate communication streams across 5 channels (4 LinkedIn Recruiter profiles via Unipile API and 1 Teamtailor ATS instance via Teamtailor API/Webhooks), processes incoming candidate messages asynchronously using an AI layer, generates contextual next-step response proposals, and feeds everything into a self-hosted Chatwoot instance.
The central inbox interface runs on Chatwoot Community Edition (deployed via Docker) utilizing API Channels. The custom middleware runs serverlessly on Vercel backed by Supabase/Neon (Postgres + pgvector), Upstash Redis, and QStash/Inngest.

## Tech Stack
Central Inbox UI: Chatwoot Community Edition (Self-Hosted via Docker Compose)

Middleware Runtime & Hosting: Vercel Serverless Functions (Node.js / Next.js Route Handlers)

Repository & CI/CD: GitHub

External APIs:

ConnectSafely.ai (LinkedIn account messaging & lifecycle)

Teamtailor API & Company Webhooks (ATS candidate profiles & messages)

Chatwoot Application REST API (Contacts, Conversations, Messages, Private Notes)

Database & Vector Store: Supabase or Neon (PostgreSQL with pgvector for candidate history & RAG)

Cache & Rate Limiting: Upstash Redis (REST-based client for serverless execution)

Async Job Queue: Upstash QStash or Inngest (prevents Vercel serverless HTTP timeouts)

AI Orchestration: Qwen3 Coder / Deepseek R1
## Critical Patterns & Conventions
- When ever possible recommend building an AI-First solution, where solution uses an AI to make the user experience easy and streamlines. 
- Prioritize using open source packages whenever you can instead of building your own. Preferred package repository is npm, but do also search for open source github repos. 
- Prioritize `@Docs` for internal documentation and `@Web` for external resources.
- Use `@Git` and `@Folders` in combination to understand the codebase structure and history.
- Structure tool calls logically, documenting the rationale before execution.
- For complex tasks, break them down into smaller, manageable steps using tool calls.
- When using `@Files` or `@Code`, specify the file path and relevant code sections clearly.
- If a tool call fails, attempt to diagnose the issue using error messages and logs. Refer to the troubleshooting guides if needed:
    - @Common Issues
    - @Troubleshooting Guide
- Follow consistent naming conventions (e.g., camelCase for variables, PascalCase for classes).
- Use meaningful variable and function names.
- Write clear and concise comments.
- Break down complex functions into smaller, more manageable units.
- Handle errors gracefully and provide informative error messages.
- Write unit and integration tests for critical components.
- Keep code DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself) by abstracting reusable logic into functions or modules.

## Constraints
- You have a limited context window (Claude's limitation).
- You can make up to 25 tool calls per turn in Agent Composer mode.
- Adhere to the "Cursor AI Operating Instructions" provided separately.
- Prioritize code quality, maintainability, and security.
- Avoid making assumptions; ask clarifying questions when needed.

